        "subtitle": "Eight short facts about food",
        "text": "A  **Brown or white?** Many people think that eggs with brown shells are better for you than eggs with white shells. In fact, there is no difference inside the egg, whatever its colour. The colour of the shell depends on the kind of hen that laid the egg: Rhode Island Reds, for example, lay brown-shelled eggs, while Leghorns lay white-shelled eggs. All eggs are good for you, whatever the colour of their shells.\n\nB  **A fishy story.** People who live inside the Arctic Circle eat about 160 kilograms of fish a year! People in Norway eat about 45 kilograms. Even though Australia is surrounded by sea, Australians do not eat nearly as much fish: only about seven kilograms a year.\n\nC  **Vitamin C.** When Scott set off on his expedition to the South Pole in 1902, he took plenty of food to stop his party going hungry, but he forgot to take anything that provided vitamin C. The extremely cold weather gave the men frostbite, and the frostbite did not heal. It actually became much worse, because there was no vitamin C in their diet.\n\nD  **Beefing it up.** The beef used in McDonald's hamburgers around the world each year weighs about three times as much as the giant cruise ship Queen Elizabeth II. If all the hamburgers sold in the world in a year were placed end to end, they would reach from the Earth to the Moon and back more than 30 times.\n\nE  **Sniff, sniff.** We can smell far more substances than we can taste. If you have a cold and a blocked nose, there are some foods you cannot taste because you cannot smell them. In a series of experiments, people were blindfolded, their noses were completely blocked, and they were given coffee, chocolate and garlic. They had no idea what they were eating!\n\nF  **Busy bees.** Honey tastes good to us, but it is really food for bees. For every kilogram of honey taken from a commercial hive, the bees in the hive use about eight kilograms. To collect enough nectar for that extra kilogram taken by humans, bees fly a total distance equal to about six times round the Earth. No wonder they are called \"busy little bees\"!\n\nG  **An apple a day …** Apples (and many other fruits and vegetables) go brown once they are cut and exposed to the air. This is because they contain an enzyme that reacts with the oxygen in the air and turns the flesh yellowish brown, then brown. If you brush the cut surface with lemon juice, which is acidic, the enzyme cannot work as well, and the apple will not go brown for several hours.\n\nH  **Bread.** Wholemeal bread is made from the whole of the wheat grain and is light brown. White bread is made from wheat with some of the outer brown layers removed. Brown bread is somewhere in between, because it is made from a mixture of wholemeal and white flour. In some countries colourings can be added to make bread look brown; other countries do not allow this. All bread is good for you, but wholemeal is the best, because it contains more fibre, more vitamins and more minerals than brown or white bread."
